Chapter-SSH Management
=========================
1. How passwordess authentication established in linux ?

Step 1: Generate SSH Key (Client Side)
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096
         Creates:
            ~/.ssh/id_rsa       # private key (SECRET)
            ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub   # public key (SHARED)

Step 2: Copy Public Key to Server
ssh-copy-id user@server
         Public key gets added to:
            ~/.ssh/authorized_keys   # on server and server looks into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys at the time of connection from client

Step 3: Set Proper Permissions (IMPORTANT)
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

2. What all parameters we can modify in /etc/ssh/sshd_config file ?

PasswordAuthentication no        # disable password login (force key-based auth)
PubkeyAuthentication yes         # enable SSH key-based login
PermitRootLogin no               # disable direct root login
AllowUsers user1 user2           # allow only specific users
DenyUsers baduser                # block specific users

Port 2222                        # change default SSH port (default 22)
MaxAuthTries 3                   # limit login attempts
LoginGraceTime 30                # timeout for login

AuthorizedKeysFile .ssh/authorized_keys   # location of public keys
StrictModes yes                  # enforce correct permissions on SSH files

UsePAM yes                       # enable PAM authentication (Linux auth system)
ChallengeResponseAuthentication no   # disable challenge-response auth

AllowGroups devops               # allow only specific groups
DenyGroups testgrp              # block specific groups

LogLevel INFO                   # logging level
SyslogFacility AUTHPRIV         # log facility

3. How to disable SSH root login completely ?
Edit SSH config:
PermitRootLogin no         # disables root login via SSH
Apply changes:
systemctl restart sshd

4. How to allow only key-based authentication (no password) ?
Edit SSH config:
PasswordAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
Restart SSH:
systemctl restart sshd

5. How SSH works internally during authentication ?
Step 1: Client sends request to server
Step 2: Server checks ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Step 3: Server sends challenge
Step 4: Client signs with private key
Step 5: Server verifies using public key
Step 6: Access granted

6. How to troubleshoot SSH connection issues ?
Check SSH service:
systemctl status sshd
Check logs:
tail -f /var/log/secure        # RHEL/CentOS
tail -f /var/log/auth.log      # Ubuntu
Test verbose:
