3. Compare TCP and UDP
TC V/S UDP

TCP → requires connection (3-way handshake)
UDP → no connection (direct send)

Reliability
TCP → reliable (guaranteed delivery)
UDP → unreliable (no guarantee)

Data Order
TCP → maintains order
UDP → no order guarantee

Speed
TCP → slower (checks + acknowledgments)
UDP → faster (no overhead)

Error Checking
TCP → error detection + recovery
UDP → only basic checksum (no recovery)

Acknowledgement
TCP → yes (ACK required)
UDP → no ACK

Packet Control
TCP → flow & congestion control
UDP → no control

Header Size
TCP → 20–60 bytes (more overhead)
UDP → 8 bytes (lightweight)

TCP → use when data must be correct (login, DB, APIs)
UDP → use when speed matters (video, gaming, DNS)

