**Chapter-Kernel Management**
=========================
1. What is Kernel in Linux Operating System?

Kernel is the core (heart) of the OS. It acts as a bridge between Hardware and Software. It is the first component loaded when the system boots

It manages:
CPU
Memory (RAM)
Devices (disk, network, etc.)
Networking
Security

Applications do not directly talk to hardware
They use system calls → handled by kernel

2. How many type of kernel are there ?
There are mainly 5 types of kernels:

Monolithic Kernel
-- All services run in kernel space
   Fast but large
   Example: Linux Kernel

Microkernel
-- Minimal kernel, services run in user space
   More secure and modular
   Example: Minix

Hybrid Kernel
-- Combination of monolithic + microkernel
   Example: Windows, macOS

Exokernel
-- Very minimal, gives more control to applications

Modular Kernel (Linux specific concept)
-- Supports loading/unloading modules dynamically
   Example: Device drivers using modprobe

Linux = Monolithic + Modular (best answer)

3. What Kernel Does in Linux OS?
Kernel is responsible for core system functionality:
-- Process Management
-- Memory Management
-- Device Management
-- File System Management
-- System Calls Handling
-- Security & Access Control

4. What is initramfs / initrd?
-- Both are temporary root filesystems loaded into memory during startup to prepare before mounting the real root filesystem.

5. Explain linux system boot procedure ? 
-- POST → BIOS/UEFI → MBR/GPT → GRUB2 → Kernel → initramfs → systemd
-- GRUB2 → [loads kernel into RAM] → Kernel starts → [mounts initramfs as temp root] → initramfs runs

| Your Sequence | Detailed Step | What Happens                                               |
| ------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| POST          | Step 1        | Hardware self-test by BIOS/UEFI                            |
| BIOS/UEFI     | Step 1        | Firmware loads, finds bootable device                      |
| MBR/GPT       | Step 2        | Boot sector where bootloader lives                         |
| GRUB2         | Step 2–3      | Bootloader loads kernel + initramfs into memory            |
| initramfs     | Step 4–5      | Temporary root filesystem; loads drivers, mounts real root |
| systemd       | Step 6–7      | First user-space process; starts services, shows login     |

| Step | Component | Key Action                    | More Info                                                                                                                                        |
| ---- | --------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| 1    | POST      | Hardware check                | Runs before BIOS; checks CPU, RAM, storage, keyboard, graphics; fails with error/beep if hardware faulty                                         |
| 2    | BIOS/MBR  | Load bootloader               | BIOS reads first 64 bytes of disk (MBR) or GPT; MBR contains GRUB2 code + partition table; UEFI uses .EFI file instead                           |
| 3    | GRUB2     | Load kernel + initramfs       | Located in /boot/grub2/; config at /etc/default/grub; loads vmlinuz-* and initramfs-*.img into RAM; displays boot menu                           |
| 4    | Kernel    | Initialize, mount temp root   | File: /boot/vmlinuz-<version>; decompresses itself; loads built-in drivers; mounts initramfs as tmpfs in RAM (temp root)                         |
| 5    | initramfs | Load drivers, mount real root | File: /boot/initramfs-<version>.img; contains kernel modules (drivers); runs /init script; uses switch_root to switch to real /; discarded after |
| 6    | systemd   | Start services, show login    | PID 1; default target: /etc/systemd/system/default.target; mounts /etc/fstab; starts sshd, cron, NetworkManager; TTY or GUI login                |

